- {"id": "VER-CAS-06", "criterion": "A spec that no longer covers the new version is rewritten to it, keeping the operator and precision for Cargo, npm and Python (`^1.2` becomes `^2.0` for 2.0.0, and `~=1.2` becomes `~=2.0`), or to the exact version for npm when `preserve-npm-ranges = false`. A runtime, optional, build or peer dependent that is itself released in this run, for any reason, has every such spec on a bumped internal dependency raised to the dependency's new version the same way, even when the spec still covers it (a covering spec that cannot be raised, such as a wildcard or a bare Python requirement, is left unchanged); a dependent that is not releasing keeps its spec unless out 
- {"id": "VER-CAS-07", "criterion": "A rewrite uses the source declaration's manifest ecosystem and preserves its declared dependency key or spelling, independent of either package's display prefix. A Cargo dependency's `package` name resolves its native target while edits retain its alias; inherited aliases resolve the declaration in the Cargo workspace that governs the member. Python distribution names match using PEP 503 normalization while edits retain the spelling in the dependency declaration. A Cargo dependency inherited with `workspace = true` is rewritten in the workspace root's `[workspace.dependencies]`, not in the member manifest."}
- {"id": "VER-CAS-08", "criterion": "A version or dependency-range change to a `package.json` replaces only the string literals that changed: every other byte of the file, including inline arrays and objects, escape sequences, line endings and a byte order mark, is as the author wrote it. A change that adds or removes a key rewrites the file with key order, indentation, line endings, byte order mark and trailing newline kept. A Python or Cargo dependency edit keeps the comments written above and after it, including a comment above a full-table Cargo `version` key in `[workspace.dependencies]`."}
- {"id": "VER-CAS-09", "criterion": "A Python dependency range on a package released with its dependent is rewritten to cover the new version: `==`, `~=` and `>=` keep their operator; a two-clause `>=X,<Y` range becomes `>=NEW` with an upper bound one minor above NEW when the original spanned a single minor (`>=0.1.0,<0.2`) and the next major otherwise; and a Poetry caret or tilde becomes the equivalent PEP 440 range around NEW. A pre-release target is not written into a caret or tilde. A spec that still covers the new version but cannot be raised is left unchanged without a diagnostic (VER-CAS-06); a runtime, optional, build or peer spec that does not cover it fails with E319; only a developm
- {"id": "VER-CAS-10", "criterion": "The cascade aborts with E105 when it does not settle within 4 x (package count) + 1 steps, and with a grammar-mismatch error when a spec and the new version use different version grammars (SemVer vs PEP 440).", "is_error_case": true}
- {"id": "VER-GRP-01", "criterion": "All package members of a `[[fixed-group]]` receive the group's highest severity and one shared version, computed the same grammar- and pre-release-aware way as any other bump (VER-PRE-03): the alignment base is the on-disk version of the group's released member (a member with a release tag) if the group has one, otherwise the highest on-disk version among its members, bumped by that severity."}
- {"id": "VER-GRP-02", "criterion": "A fixed group whose released members have different on-disk versions aborts the run with E106, one whose released members use different version grammars (or whose untagged members do) aborts with E107, one whose released member has no on-disk base version aborts with E205, and one with no live member carrying a base version aborts with E206. A group member that is not a workspace package aborts the run with E108 when the group is loaded, so E205 and E206 are defensive checks.", "is_error_case": true}
- {"id": "VER-GRP-06", "criterion": "A computed bump \u2014 for a single package or a fixed group's shared target \u2014 that would sort behind the version it was bumped from aborts the run with E207 instead of writing the regressed version to disk. A fixed group's version is judged against its alignment base (VER-GRP-01), so an untagged member that has never been released moves onto the released member's line even when its own on-disk version is higher.", "is_error_case": true}
- {"id": "VER-GRP-03", "criterion": "When any member of a `[[linked-group]]` is bumped, every member receives the group's highest severity and all members move to the highest version any member's own bump produces (members at 1.4.0 and 2.7.3 with a minor bump both become 2.8.0)."}
- {"id": "VER-GRP-04", "criterion": "A package claimed by two groups is rejected: E109 when the claims use different spellings of the same package, and a group-conflict error when they use the same spelling.", "is_error_case": true}
- {"id": "VER-GRP-05", "criterion": "An attached platform package, or a fixed-group member naming a platform manifest, is not bumped as a package: when its owner is bumped, the platform manifest gets the owner's new version and the owner's `optionalDependencies` entry for it is pinned to that version. A co-located platform manifest that is also one of the owner's canonical manifests is written once, as part of the owner's own bump."}
- {"id": "VER-PRE-01", "criterion": "`callisto pre enter <tag>` writes `pre.json` in the changesets directory (default `.changeset/pre.json`) with `mode` `pre`, the tag, every package's current version under `initialVersions`, keyed by its display id (`foo`, or `cargo/foo` when another ecosystem has a `foo`) and an empty `changesets` list, then stages the file with git; under `--dry-run` it prints the content and writes nothing. For promoted packages, an older bare key is assigned to the unique historical owner at the commit that added pre.json; if no unique owner exists, version fails with E245."}
- {"id": "VER-PRE-02", "criterion": "`pre enter` fails when the tag is empty or whitespace, or when `.changeset/pre.json` exists with mode `pre` (also under `--dry-run`); when it exists with mode `exit`, `pre enter` instead resumes the cycle under the new tag, keeping its recorded `initialVersions` and `changesets`. `callisto pre exit` sets `mode` to `exit` and fails when the file is missing or already in `exit` mode.", "is_error_case": true}
- {"id": "VER-PRE-03", "criterion": "In pre mode, a bumped package's version is its `initialVersions` entry bumped by its severity plus `-{tag}.N`, where N starts at 0 and increments while the release part is unchanged (0.1.0 with a minor changeset and tag `beta` gives 0.2.0-beta.0, then 0.2.0-beta.1); PEP 440 packages use `aN` for tags `a`/`alpha`, `bN` for `b`/`beta`, `rcN` for `rc`/`c`/`pre`/`preview` (case-insensitive), and `.devN` for any other tag."}
- {"id": "VER-PRE-04", "criterion": "In pre mode, `version` never deletes a changeset from disk. A changeset whose id is not yet in `pre.json`'s `changesets` list counts toward severity, produces a changelog entry, and has its id added to `changesets`; a changeset whose id is already there is not pending again: a package named only by recorded changesets is not bumped, so a rerun with nothing new changes nothing, and a package named by a new changeset still counts the recorded ones toward its severity. The `allow-empty-changesets`/`--strict` handling from VER-AGG-06 triggers when no changeset in pre mode is new."}
- {"id": "VER-PRE-05", "criterion": "After `pre exit`, the next `version` moves every package at a prerelease version to its stable release (0.2.0-beta.1 becomes 0.2.0), consumes and deletes the pending changesets, and deletes `pre.json`."}
- {"id": "VER-PRE-06", "criterion": "A malformed `.changeset/pre.json` (invalid JSON, a missing `mode`, `tag`, `initialVersions` or `changesets` field, a mode other than `pre` or `exit`, or an unparsable version) makes `version` fail with E114 and write nothing; an unreadable file fails with E115.", "is_error_case": true}
- {"id": "VER-PRE-07", "criterion": "`callisto snapshot --tag <tag>` sets every workspace package, and every attached platform package and fixed-group platform manifest, to a snapshot version built from the same tag and sha7 (the first 7 characters of HEAD) in the package's own grammar: `0.0.0-{tag}-{sha7}` for a SemVer package, `0.0.0.dev0+{tag}.{sha7}` for a Pep440 package; it fails if HEAD cannot be resolved. A snapshot version is never tagged or published: `release` refuses it with E169."}
- {"id": "VER-PRE-08", "criterion": "A snapshot writes manifests and refreshes the lockfile of each active ecosystem (creating a missing `Cargo.lock` but never staging it), unconditionally (there is no `--no-refresh-lockfiles` for `snapshot`), so a subsequent locked install resolves against the snapshot version; it ignores and keeps pending changesets, writes no changelog or `pre.json`, and stages nothing in git; under `--dry-run` it writes nothing."}
- {"id": "VER-PRE-09", "criterion": "For every workspace dependency whose current spec does not cover the snapshot version, `snapshot` rewrites that spec using the source declaration's manifest ecosystem and declared key or spelling, including Cargo aliases. Inherited Cargo declarations are rewritten in the root manifest of the Cargo workspace that governs the member rather than the member manifest. A spec that already covers the snapshot version is left unchanged."}
- {"id": "VER-PRE-10", "criterion": "Every `pre.json` initial version is validated using the owning package's native grammar after historical and promoted identity resolution; the original JSON string is validated, not a spelling normalized by a cross-ecosystem fallback. A malformed owned baseline fails with E114 before version, changelog, pre-state or changeset writes during `version`, `pre exit` or resuming with `pre enter`."}
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-01", "criterion": "`version` writes the new version to every canonical manifest of a bumped package, so both manifests of a dual-manifest package move together."}
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-02", "criterion": "Before writing a package or platform manifest, apply reads its current version: equal to the planned `from` it is written, equal to the target it is left unchanged, so applying the same plan again is safe."}
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-03", "criterion": "A manifest whose current version is neither the planned `from` nor the target aborts apply with E117 naming the path, the found version and both expected versions.", "is_error_case": true}
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-04", "criterion": "A manifest that receives a version bump and dependency rewrites in the same run is read once and written once with all changes, and a manifest already at its target with no rewrites is not rewritten. An owner's `optionalDependencies` pins, and edits to a workspace root `Cargo.toml` that is also a package manifest, are written separately."}
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-05", "criterion": "Files are written one at a time, each by atomic replacement, so an interrupted run never leaves a partially written file; a failing write stops the run and leaves earlier files in place. A write also fails when the directory holding the new file cannot be synced, unless the filesystem cannot sync a directory or the directory cannot be opened for reading (permission denied)."}
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-17", "criterion": "Before the first write, every file the plan edits, creates or deletes is checked: a manifest must exist as a readable regular file, no path may be a symbolic link or sit beneath one (Git cannot stage it), no path may leave the workspace, the directory of each written file (or its nearest existing parent) must take a new file, and a file the plan stages must not be ignored by a .gitignore unless Git already tracks it. Checking creates no file or directory. Any failure stops the run with E314 naming every such path, and nothing has been written, staged or deleted. A lockfile that a .gitignore ignores is still refreshed on disk but is not staged. The checks 
- {"id": "VER-READ-01", "criterion": "Only a missing file is treated as absent when reading `callisto.toml`, `.changeset/pre.json`, a changelog, or the changeset directory. A file that cannot be read or inspected (a permission error, a directory, invalid UTF-8) fails the command naming the path; a dangling link or an entry removed during listing holds no changeset, and a directory entry named like a changeset inside the changeset directory is skipped.", "is_error_case": true}
- {"id": "VER-READ-02", "criterion": "Only \"not found\" is treated as absent when callisto asks Git about a tag, ref, path or file content: a tag list, a ref that does not resolve, a path missing at a revision, a search with no match, and a repository with no commits yet each answer \"none\". Any other Git failure (not a repository, a broken repository, a revision that cannot be searched) fails the command naming the Git command and its error. A tag that is listed but does not resolve to a commit fails `status` and `version` instead of widening commit inference to the whole history.", "is_error_case": true}
- {"id": "VER-READ-04", "criterion": "Planning fails with E315 in a shallow clone when any package infers its bump from commits (`release-trigger = \"auto\"`), because the commit window would be shorter than the real one. Packages that take their bump from changesets alone are unaffected.", "is_error_case": true}
- {"id": "VER-READ-03", "criterion": "`status` reports a tag that matches a package's tag template but is not a valid version as one warning naming the tag, however many packages share the template.", "is_error_case": true}
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-18", "criterion": "A file callisto rewrites keeps its permission bits, and a file it creates gets the mode any new file gets under the process umask."}
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-06", "criterion": "For each bumped package, apply prepends a `## {version}` section under the `# {package}` heading of its changelog (default `CHANGELOG.md` in the package directory, overridable with `changelog`), creating the file if missing and keeping CRLF line endings; a changelog that already has that heading is left unchanged."}
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-07", "criterion": "A changelog section lists each changeset summary as a bullet (nonblank continuation lines indented and blank lines empty), an inferred bump as a commit subject with its short sha, a group bump as \"Released together with the `{group}` {fixed|linked} group.\", a new fixed-group member as \"Joined the `{group}` group at this version.\", a peer escalation as \"Peer dependency `{dep}` requires `{version}`\", and cascaded bumps as \"`{dep}` \u2192 `{version}`\" lines under a \"Dependency updates\" bullet."}
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-08", "criterion": "`version` refreshes affected lockfiles and stages written manifests, changelogs, `pre.json` changes and existing lockfiles before consuming release inputs; in pre mode it consumes no changesets. It stages consumed changeset and `pre.json` deletions via `git rm --cached --ignore-unmatch` before unlinking those files from disk. If refresh, generated-state staging or input-deletion staging fails, changesets and pre-state deletion inputs remain on disk for retry."}
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-09", "criterion": "`version --dry-run` prints the computed plan (text output starts with `[DRY-RUN]`) and writes, deletes and stages nothing."}
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-10", "criterion": "`version --emit-decision <path>` writes the release decision JSON to `<path>` relative to the workspace root after the plan has been applied successfully: `schemaVersion` 2, a digest, and one entry per canonical manifest of each bumped package (ecosystem-qualified name, target version, reasons) sorted by package. Reason `kind` is `changeset`, `inference`, `fixedGroup` or `linkedGroup` (with `groupId`), `cascade` (with `from`, the source package's distribution that the dependent's dependency names, the one in the entry's own ecosystem when it names several, and `edgeKind`: `Runtime`, `Optional`, `Build` or `Peer`, or lowercase `peer` for peer escalation) o
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-11", "criterion": "`--emit-decision` with `--dry-run` is rejected before any work, and a plan with no bumps, or whose group members have different target versions, fails to produce a decision before any file is written.", "is_error_case": true}
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-12", "criterion": "A diagnostic escalated to an error (for example `--strict` on the empty-changeset warning) is evaluated right after planning: `version` writes nothing -- not the manifests and changelog, not `pre.json`, not the `--emit-decision` file -- and exits with a failure status, instead of applying the plan and only then failing.", "is_error_case": true}
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-13", "criterion": "Outside `mode: pre`, after planning but before any write, `version` refuses with E121 when a canonical manifest's on-disk effective version differs from its version at `HEAD`, or when its effective version staged in the git index differs from `HEAD`, provided a changeset remains in the `HEAD` tree or configured working-tree changesets directory (including untracked files), or a canonical manifest is staged. Staged metadata-only edits with unchanged effective versions are allowed. A Cargo package that inherits its version is compared against the workspace it names at `HEAD`, in the index and in the working tree, so a staged bump to the workspace it named a
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-14", "criterion": "By default, applying a plan refreshes and stages lockfiles for every ecosystem represented by an actual version, dependency, platform or optional-dependency write target, including all manifests of a dual-manifest package and workspace-root Cargo writes. It refreshes each existing lockfile, and creates and stages a missing `Cargo.lock`: `cargo update --workspace`, run in the directory of each Cargo workspace written to and refreshing the `Cargo.lock` beside its root manifest, for Cargo; for Pypi, `uv lock` if `uv.lock` exists, else `poetry lock --no-update` if `poetry.lock` exists, else `pdm lock --update-reuse` if `pdm.lock` exists; for Npm, `npm install
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-15", "criterion": "A lockfile refresh command that exits non-zero aborts `version` with E208 naming the lockfile and the exact command that failed, instead of recording the failure and continuing.", "is_error_case": true}
- {"id": "VER-APPLY-16", "criterion": "When E121 checks a staged canonical manifest outside prerelease mode, `version` compares its effective version in the Git index as well as the working tree against `HEAD`. It refuses if either copy contains an uncommitted version change, even when the other copy matches `HEAD`; staged metadata-only changes with the same effective version remain allowed.", "is_error_case": true}
